      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Piperazine is a secondary heterocyclic amine that may give rise to occupational asthma of uncertain mechanism."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We report on a 42-year-old woman, a process operator in a chemical factory, who developed work-related symptoms of rhinitis and asthma upon exposure to piperazine citrate. She remained symptom free during holidays and days off work."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Skin prick test with piperazine citrate was positive. Specific inhalation challenge with piperazine citrate at a concentration of 5 mg/m3 for 30 minutes elicited an isolated late asthmatic response. Airway hyperresponsiveness to methacholine significantly increased 3 hours after the piperazine challenge, preceding the late asthmatic response."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "This patient had developed occupational asthma caused by piperazine, as confirmed by the specific inhalation challenge test, possibly due to an immunological mechanism."
        }
      ]},
